The '''Outland TIE fighter''' was a class of [[TIE series|TIE fighter series]] [[starfighter]] that was designed as the [[Galactic Empire]] was losing its territory. Otherwise congruent to the appearance of a standard [[TIE/ln space superiority starfighter]], the Outland TIE fighter's wings folded when landing on a levelled surface, allowing for ease of access to the cockpit and creating a distinctive form not dissimilar to that of an [[X-wing starfighter]] with its [[S-foils]] in an open position. In addition, landing gears were concealed inside the bottom of the cockpit to allow for ease of use and stability. The personal starfighter of [[Moff]] [[Gideon]], at least [[Gideon's Outland TIE fighter|one Outland fighter]], flown by the Moff [[Gender|himself]], was used by his [[Gideon's Imperial remnant|Imperial remnant]] during the [[New Republic Era]], though the [[Mandalorian]] [[bounty hunter]] [[Din Djarin]] took down the starfighter.

==Description==
[[File:GideonTIE-TMS01E08.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The interior of an Outland TIE fighter]]
A model of the [[Sienar Fleet Systems]]' [[TIE series|TIE fighter series]], the Outland TIE fighter had model-specific landing gear similar to that of the [[T-65B X-wing starfighter|T-65B]] and [[T-70 X-wing starfighter]]s flown by the [[New Republic Defense Fleet]]. The [[starfighter]] had an outward appearance very similar to the [[TIE/ln space superiority starfighter]]<ref name="Outlaws">{{SWCT|set=Star Wars: The Mandalorian - Illustrated Outlaws|cardname=Outland TIE Fighter}}</ref> when in flight, but its [[solar collector]] panel wings had the additional capability of folding at the center to form a V-shape.<ref name="Action">{{SWCT|set=The Mandalorian - Action!|cardname=TIE Fighter}}</ref>

Once folded, it allowed the Outland TIE to land on a surface utilizing its recessed landing gear, permitting easy access to the [[pilot]] without a [[hangar]] rack armature or a specialized landing pad.<ref name="Chapter 7" /> Accordingly, the tripodal landing gear and configurable wings allowed the pilot to land on uneven ground or in wilderness conditions.<ref name="Outland TIE databank">{{Databank|outland-tie-fighter|Outland TIE fighter|nolive=1}}</ref> It also featured other accoutrements.<ref name="Mandalorian Info" />

==History==
[[File:Outland_TIE_landing.png|thumb|250px|right|Moff Gideon's Outland TIE landing on Nevarro.]]
During most of the [[Galactic Civil War]],<ref name="Outland TIE databank" /> a [[The galaxy|galactic]] conflict fought between the [[Galactic Empire]] and the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic]]/[[New Republic]],<ref name="Aftermath">[[Aftermath (novel)|''Aftermath'']]</ref> the Empire's TIE/ln space superiority [[starfighter]]s were launched from dedicated [[Imperial Military|military]] installations like well-stocked [[hangar]]s, carrier craft, and base facilities. However, as the Empire lost more of its territory, TIEs were adjusted into the Outland fighters to make it easier for the starfighter to land in non-ideal locations.<ref name="Outland TIE databank" />

At least five Outland TIE fighters were used by [[Gideon's Imperial remnant]]<ref name="Chapter 7" /> by [[9 ABY]].<ref name="Date">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]''</ref> [[Moff]] [[Gideon]], the leader of the remnant, used a [[Gideon's Outland TIE fighter|personal one]] to travel to [[Nevarro]] with [[Gideon's battalion|his forces]] and then landed the starfighter on the ground, exiting out to speak with the [[Mandalorian]] [[bounty hunter]] [[Din Djarin]] and his allies.<ref name="Chapter 7" />

[[File:GideonDarksaber-MandalorianCh8.png|thumb|left|250px|Moff Gideon stands atop his crashed Outland TIE fighter.]]
After Djarin, [[Carasynthia Dune]], [[Greef Karga]], [[IG-11]], and [[Din Grogu|the Child]] escaped the [[Nevarro school|Nevarro cantina]] and the city, Gideon used his Outland TIE fighter to engage the group with [[laser cannon]] fire. Before he returned for a second strafing run, Karga tried to get the Child to use his [[Force power]]s but the [[Force-sensitive]] was unable to. Djarin then started up his [[jetpack]] and chased the starfighter. He then used his [[grappling line]] to secure himself on the TIE fighter. Unable to force his way into the cockpit, Djarin tried to plant an explosive but the Moff jiggled it off his [[starship]]. The Mandalorian planted a second explosive on the left joint, causing the ship to spiral out of control, which bought the Outland TIE crashing to Nevarro's surface. Gideon then used the [[Darksaber]] to escape the wrecked hull.<ref name="Chapter 8" />

Outland TIE fighters were later stationed at the [[Nevarro Imperial base|Imperial base]] on Nevarro, which were destroyed by the ''[[Razor Crest]]'' during a [[Second skirmish on Nevarro|mission]] undertaken by Din, Cara, Greef, and a [[The Fledgling Mythrol|Mythrol]] to destroy the base by disabling a security feature that prevented [[Magma|lava]] underneath the base from rising.<ref name="Chapter 12" />

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|Behind me is a TIE fighter in its landing position, with landing gear out, which hasn't ever been shown before, and the wings are gonna be added in CG, where we fold them based on Doug Chiang's drawing.|Jon Favreau, on the set of ''The Mandalorian''|<ref name="Connections">{{DisneyGallery|Connections}}</ref>}}
[[File:Outland-TIE-fighter-SWCT.png|thumb|right|300px|An Outland TIE fighter.]]
The Outland TIE fighter was first depicted in the mobile app [[Star Wars: Card Trader|''Star Wars'': Card Trader]] as part of the set Illustrated Outlaws released on [[October 4]], [[2019]].<ref name="Outlaws" /> It appears in the final two episodes of the [[The Mandalorian Season One|first season]] of ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]'', a [[2019]] [[Disney+]] television series.<ref name="Chapter 8" /><ref name="Chapter 7" />

The ship was originally designed by [[Industrial Light & Magic]] concept artist [[Doug Chiang]] for the [[2015]] film [[Star Wars: Episode VII The Force Awakens|''Star Wars'': Episode VII ''The Force Awakens'']],<ref name="ILM">{{ILM|url=visual_development/the-force-awakens-portfolio|text=''The Force Awaken'' Portfolio}}</ref> though it went unused in the film itself. Later, during the development of ''The Mandalorian'', series creator [[Jon Favreau]] and his team saw Chiang's concept and decided to incorporate it into their project. To bring the vehicle to life, a physical prop of the ship was built on the show's set, whereas the TIE's folded wings were realized through computer-generated imagery.<ref name="Connections" />

